    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d210">

                <pb facs="0271" n="171" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg171"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.1211/255a</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Division of Mexican Affairs,
                        Department of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Second Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Adee</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-06-07"><hi rendition="#italic">June 7,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Adee</hi>: The Mexican Chargé d’Affaires,
                    being apprehensive that his political adversaries might attempt a forcible
                    entrance of the Embassy, asked that a special patrolman be stationed there
                    continuously.</p>
                <p>To this oral request, repeated by this Division, the Chief of Police responded
                    with alacrity.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d210-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed>C. M. J[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ohnston</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d210-f1" n="26" xml:id="d210-f1-content">
                <p>Police detail was assigned to the Mexican Embassy May 13, and withdrawn June
                    5.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
